          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Instances of “dualities” relating two different, maybe opposing, but to some extent equivalent concepts or phenomena are ubiquitous in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            (and in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualities+in+physics"} dualities in physics
          %h2#general_notions General notions
          %p
            In terms of general abstract concepts in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %h3#abstractformalaxiomatic_duality Abstract/formal/axiomatic duality
          %p
            Instances here include projective duality, duality in lattice theory, and duality in category theory. In each case, one has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature"} signature
            admits a nontrivial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            theory, the involution swaps the domain and codomain and order of composition, etc., and for any theorem of formal category theory, the corresponding dual statement is also a theorem (because the set of axioms of category theory are closed under taking formal duals).
          %p
            Such formal duality can also be expressed at the level of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            of the theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            there is a “dual” or “opposite” model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^{op}
            obtained by re-interpreting each formal sort/function/relation of the theory as the dual sort/function/relation. This further induces an involution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod(T) \to Mod(T)
            on the category of models. For example, in the case of category theory, this operation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \mapsto C^{op}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo :
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{op}: Cat \to Cat
            on the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            of (small) categories, viewing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            here as a 1-category. In fact this is the only non-trivial automorphism of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "%28infinity%2Cn%29Cat#automorphisms"} here
          %p
            This involution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo :
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{op}: Cat \to Cat
            is also known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em abstract duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            often is equivalent to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            known by other means, which makes abstract duality interesting (particularly so in cases of concrete duality, which we discuss next).
          %h3#concrete_dualities Concrete dualities
          %p
            Instances here include linear duality, Stone duality, Pontryagin duality, and projective inversions with respect to a conic hypersurface. In each such case there is some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functor"} contravariant
            process of “homming into” a suitable structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            called a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \stackrel{\sim}{\to} D
            where typically
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the category of models of one type of concrete structure, and the equivalence maps the formal categorical dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
            to a category of models
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            consisting of “dual” concrete structures.
          %p
            In other cases one might not obtain an equivalence or perfect duality, but in any case a contravariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
            of functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S: C \to D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: D \to C
            between categories which can be termed a duality of sorts, in that concepts developed in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are mapped to dual concepts in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and vice-versa. Quoting (
            %a{:href => "#LawvereRosebrugh"}> Lawvere-Rosebrugh, chapter 7
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              Not every statement will be taken into its formal dual by the process of dualizing with respect to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %blockquote
              %p space vs. quantity
            %p and of logic
            %blockquote
              %p theory vs. example
            %p
              may be considered as the detailed study of the extent to which formal duality and concrete duality into a favorite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              correspond or fail to correspond. (p. 122)
          %p Some examples follow.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In linear duality, say for vector spaces over a field
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi W
                          %mo *
                        %mo =
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi W
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^\ast = \hom(W, k)
                = succeed "-linear" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\ast: Vect_k \to Vect_k
                that is adjoint to itself, in that there is a double-dual embedding
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mi W
                      %mo :
                      %mi W
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_W: W \to W^{\ast\ast}
                such that
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi W
                            %mo *
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mo *
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi δ
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi W
                                %mo *
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo *
                          %mo *
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi δ
                            %mi W
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo *
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{W^\ast} = (W^\ast \stackrel{\delta_{W^\ast}}{\to} W^{\ast\ast\ast} \stackrel{(\delta_W)^\ast}{\to} W^\ast).
              %p
                This becomes a perfect duality if we restrict to finite-dimensional vector spaces, i.e., the contravariant functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi fd
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi fd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\ast: Vect_{fd} \to Vect_{fd}
                is adjoint-equivalent to itself (i.e., the covariant functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi fd
                      %mo →
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi fd
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((-)^\ast)^{op}: Vect_{fd} \to Vect_{fd}^{op}
                is left adjoint to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi fd
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi fd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\ast: Vect_{fd}^{op} \to Vect_{fd}
                and the adjunction is an
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
            %li
              %p
                More generally, given a (usually
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+closed+category"} monoidal closed category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                induces an operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,D] : \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}^{op}
                obtained by forming the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                into
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "-dual" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mi C
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_C: C \to [ [C, D], D]
                obtained as the composite
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to [ [C, D], C \otimes [C, D]] \to [ [C, D], [C, D] \otimes C] \to [ [C, D], D]
              %p
                where the first arrow uses the unit of an adjunction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (- \otimes B) \dashv [B, -]
                where
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = [C, D]
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi B
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \otimes B \cong B \otimes C
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (- \otimes C) \dashv [C, -]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\ast \coloneqq [-, D]: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}
                is again dual to itself, exactly as in the case of linear duality above, where we have a triangular equation
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mo *
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mi C
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{C^\ast} = (\delta_C)^\ast \circ \delta_{C^\ast}
              %p
                for an adjunction
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mi op
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-, D]^{op} \dashv [-, D]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [ [-, D], D]: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}
                is an equivalence; see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object+in+a+closed+category"} dualizing object in a closed category
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mo =
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = I
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} dual object
                in the sense of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biclosed+monoidal+category"} biclosed monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                More general still is a concrete duality induced by a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi V
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: \mathcal{C} \to Set, \qquad V: \mathcal{D} \to Set
              %p
                (and often when one says “concrete”, one intends that these functors be faithful as well, so that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{D}
                can be viewed as “sets with structure”; see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} stuff, structure, property
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \in \mathcal{C}, D \in \mathcal{D}
                together with an isomorphism
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                        %mi C
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi V
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega: U C \cong V D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(-, C): C^{op} \to Set
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(-, D): D^{op} \to Set
                lift to a contravariant adjunction between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object#ambi"} here
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object#natural"} here
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+concrete+categories"} topological concrete categories
          %p Again, in all of these examples, one can consider the further condition of “perfect duality” where the units and counits of the (lifted) adjunctions are isomorphisms.
          %h3#Adjunctions Adjunctions
          %p
            Perhaps the loosest general notion of “duality” is that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lambek81"}> Lambek 81
            ). Here one may omit any concretizations via functors to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            Nevertheless, many adjunctions come packaged in “dual pairs”. A famous slogan from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
            is that “all concepts are Kan extensions”, and in that light the dual pairs are instances of the general dual pair “(right Kan extension, left Kan extension)” which are formal duals in the axiomatic sense described earlier. Via the many incarnations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} end
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
          %p
            When the adjoint functors are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            and hence
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+of+opposites"} duality of opposites
          %p
            Again, adjunctions and specifically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+adjunctions"} dual adjunctions
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+connections"}> Galois connections
            ”) may be thought of as generalized dualities, more general than “perfect duality” which involves equivalences between categories (“Galois correspondences”). However, it should also be noted that any such adjunction (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+adjunction"} dual adjunction
            %em maximal (dual) equivalence
            between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategories"} subcategories
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"}> dual object
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object+in+a+closed+category"} dualizing object in a closed category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+space"}> dual space
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual vector space
            %li
              %p
                the duality between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
                for finite dimensional (oriented) closed manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spanier-Whitehead+duality"}> Spanier-Whitehead duality
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown-Comenetz+duality"} Brown-Comenetz duality
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anderson+duality"} Anderson duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin+duality"} Pontryagin duality
                for commutative (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"}> Hausdorff
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological groups
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartier+duality"} Cartier duality
                of a finite flat commutative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+scheme"} group scheme
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+duality"} Serre duality
                on nonsingular projective algebraic varieties which has as a special case the statement of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Roch+theorem"} Riemann-Roch theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+duality"}> Grothendieck duality
                ,
                %span.newWikiWord
                  coherent duality
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/coherent+duality"} ?
                for
                %span.newWikiWord
                  coherent sheaves
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/coherentsheave"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier duality
                for abelian categories of sheaves; e.g. for a category of sheaves of abelian groups.
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Artin-Verdier duality
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Artin-Verdier+duality"} ?
                generalizing
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Tate duality
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Tate+duality"} ?
                for constructible sheaves over the spectrum of a ring of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+number"} algebraic numbers
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul+duality"} Koszul duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax+-+semantics+duality"} syntax - semantics duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+duality"} Eckmann-Hilton duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Roos-Oberst+duality"} Gabriel-Roos-Oberst duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochster+duality"} Hochster duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dowker+duality"} Dowker duality
          %h2#dualizing_objects Dualizing objects
          %p
            Of particular interest are concrete dualities between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+categories"} concrete categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C, D
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : C \to Set
          %p
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat a \in D
            with the same underlying set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a) = \hat f(\hat a)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+objects"} dualizing objects
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
